<div class="page">
<header class="masthead">
<p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; post-mortem &middot; final report &middot; 29 October 2026</p>
<h1>The post-mortem of 28 October</h1>
<p class="standfirst">This is the final report of the post-mortem of 28 October. The post-mortem studied the failed rewrite of POSITIONS entries on 26 October. The report says what the post-mortem found and what was done about it, up to the landing of the corrections and your answers on the evening of 29 October. It also says what the work taught, what it cost and what is still open.</p>
<p class="standfirst">Each section has a Select button beside its heading. The button selects the section's text for Speak. It leaves out the references at the section's end.</p>
</header>

<nav class="parts" aria-label="Sections">
<h2>Sections</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="#how">How to read this</a></li>
<li><a href="#short">In short</a></li>
<li><a href="#s1">1. What happened on 26 and 27 October</a></li>
<li><a href="#s2">2. What the post-mortem found</a></li>
<li><a href="#s3">3. What was done</a></li>
<li><a href="#s4">4. Your rulings on the account's five open decisions</a></li>
<li><a href="#s5">5. What it taught</a></li>
<li><a href="#s6">6. What it cost</a></li>
<li><a href="#s7">7. What is still open</a></li>
<li><a href="#s8">8. This folder</a></li>
</ol>
</nav>

<section id="how" class="part">
<h2>How to read this</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li>Times are in UTC. Your clock is one hour ahead of UTC.</li>
<li>The text gives each of your messages to the minute. In the references, a time with seconds, such as 13:00:30, names a message in the session transcript. The script show.py prints any message of yours by its time.</li>
<li>“The account” is the analysis of 28 October, in the file account.md beside this report. The evidence files are in the folder evidence beside it. The references name each file by its name and link to it.</li>
<li>A sentence that starts “My reading:” gives the judgement of this report's writer, an Opus worker. Every other sentence states what a cited source says.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Words used throughout</h3>
<dl class="gloss">
<dt>POSITIONS</dt><dd>Record of your decisions, one entry each, such as P-37.</dd>
<dt>FACTS</dt><dd>Record of what is established about the repository's tree, one entry each, such as F-293.</dd>
<dt>history file</dt><dd>File beside POSITIONS or FACTS. It keeps every earlier text of an entry and why the text changed.</dd>
<dt>coordinator</dt><dd>Coordinating session.</dd>
<dt>worker</dt><dd>Agent that the coordinator launches.</dd>
<dt>Sonnet, Opus and Fable</dt><dd>Model tiers.</dd>
<dt>compaction</dt><dd>Replacement of the session's context with a summary.</dd>
<dt>boot</dt><dd>Session's start-up reading after a compaction.</dd>
<dt>page</dt><dd>Web page that the coordinator publishes for you to read and comment on.</dd>
</dl>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Point 2: <a href="@G@explorations/coordinator/curator-profile/show.py">show.py</a>.</li>
<li>Point 3: <a href="@G@@PM@account.md">account.md</a>; <a href="@T@@PM@evidence">the folder evidence</a>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="short" class="part">
<h2>In short</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p><strong>What failed.</strong> On 26 October a run of the clarity workflow rewrote 13 POSITIONS entries. You rejected every text. The clarity workflow is the tool that rewrites record entries so that each entry reads on its own and keeps its meaning.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Why.</strong> The steps up to the run were right. The coordinator wrote a brief for the run, the instructions that the run worked from. That brief made a worker's list binding over the tool's own rules. On 29 October the post-mortem found a deeper cause. The coordinator took a worker's report as the plan. No judgement stood between the report and the action.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What was done.</strong> At your word, the coordinator restored the tool and gave it an input for the review's findings. The review is the phase 3 exit review, which section 1 describes. A finding is a point that the review raised about an entry. The corrections ran first as a pilot of four entries. A pilot is a small first run. They then ran as two chunks of seven entries each. A chunk is a group of entries. Your reading found the next fault. Every finding had gone into the entry's text. An Opus worker then sorted the findings by home and by weight. A finding's home is the place in the record where it belongs. Its weight is how much it matters. One Opus writer wrote the changes from your comments. The changes landed, that is, entered the main branch, at 13:02 on 29 October.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What it taught.</strong> A small pilot that you read was the only check of form that worked. Each finding has a home, and the entry's text is only one of them. A review that reports every fault needs a sort by significance before anyone writes a text.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What is open.</strong> Part 2 of the salvage is open. The salvage is the set of small, targeted corrections that keep the review's sound work. Three of your answers of the evening are not yet in the record. The changes to the skills, the instruction files that the sessions load, wait on their branch. Some upkeep remains. Section 7 lists it all.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">Section 1</span>What happened on 26 and 27 October</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p>The phase 3 exit review is the review that you ordered on 24 October, before the switch-over. Phase 3 is the phase of the work that comes before the switch-over. The switch-over is the step at which the compiled path starts to use the interpreter's library. The compiled path compiles a program to bytecode and runs it. On 26 October the review's foundations round checked the nine tenets against the team's sources. The nine tenets are the project's base decisions. The team is the Sun Labs designers of Fortress.</p></li>
<li><p>At 07:42 you approved changes to two entries. P-37 holds that there is one library, the interpreter's. P-26 holds that the team's later word wins over the specification's early text. You said: “Yes, to both rewording of or refinements of P37 and P26. But we have like a clarity process workflow that you can use for writing it with a code reader.” You dictated that message, so “code reader” stands for “cold reader”, a reader with no context. At 07:54 you agreed to the wider plan for the entries that the review's judgement would flag: “Yeah, this sounds like a solid plan.”</p></li>
<li><p>An automatic compaction came at 08:29. After it, the coordinator wrote a run brief of 7,700 characters for 13 entries. It also added a hook to the tool's script, so that the run brief overrode the tool's own briefs. The tool's own briefs are the instructions that the tool gives its agents. The run brief said: “Where they differ, this brief wins.” The coordinator launched the run at 08:38.</p></li>
<li><p>At 19:08 you rejected the texts: “It is in the Claude speak. It is not in the ASD-STE100 register. It is not written as a timeless position.” The run wrote about 2.7 million tokens. The estimate was 1.5 million.</p></li>
<li><p>On 27 October you asked to keep the review's sound work as small, targeted corrections. The record calls these corrections the salvage. You refused a new process: “No. No go. The plan seems to reinvent something for which we already have workflows.” At your word, the coordinator removed the hook.</p></li>
<li><p>On 28 October at 07:37 you said: “We already invented this process. We perfected it. It worked well during the last record consolidation. We have probably failed to encode how to use it correctly next time.” A record consolidation is a pass that rewrites the record's entries. At 07:42 you asked: “Our previous record consolidation was a success. This last one was an utter failure. What went wrong?”</p></li>
</ol>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Point 1: <a href="@G@@EV@exit-review-records.md">exit-review-records.md</a>.</li>
<li>Point 2: your messages at 07:42:15 and 07:54:36 on 26 October; <a href="@G@@EV@scope-recheck.md">scope-recheck.md</a>.</li>
<li>Point 3: commit <code>bedc80b01</code>; <a href="@G@@EV@brief-genesis.md">brief-genesis.md</a>.</li>
<li>Point 4: your message at 19:08:27 on 26 October; <a href="@G@@PM@account.md">the account</a>, sections 1 and 8.</li>
<li>Point 5: your messages at 07:12:48 and 07:47:43 on 27 October; commit <code>18788035c</code>.</li>
<li>Point 6: your messages at 07:37:43 and 07:42:25 on 28 October.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s2" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">Section 2</span>What the post-mortem found</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p><strong>You corrected the first account twice.</strong> At 09:56 you said that the question of the skills starts at the last good consolidation, not at 23 October. At 10:28 you said: “the events that happened here were all approved and correct, even in retrospect. We are analyzing what had bent the briefing that the coordinator produced.” The post-mortem revised the account after each correction.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The causes, heaviest first.</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>The Fable worker that judged the review had listed, for each entry, what the new text must make the coordinator do. The run brief made that list binding over the clarity writer's rule “Keep the meaning.” The clarity writer is the tool's step that writes the new text. The list carried batch numbers, counts and dates. The new texts grew to 1.9 times the size of the entries, and they carried history.</li>
<li>The coordinator wrote the brief at a fresh boot, eight minutes after a compaction. Its context then held 466 thousand tokens. It wrote the brief from the plan in the boot note. The boot note is the file that tells the coordinator, after a compaction or a stop, what is in flight and what to do next. Nobody checked the brief against the record's rules for an entry. The brief set no length cap and no register.</li>
<li>The skills named no tool for correcting entries after a review. The lessons that would have stopped the run stayed in four other places: a tool's brief, a README, a commit message and the held list. The held list is the file of points that you raised while reading.</li>
<li>Nothing caught the fault before the end. There was no pilot and no length check. The trap test asks whether a reader who acts as the coordinator decides right from the entries. It checks decisions only. It scored the rejected texts 12 of 12.</li>
<li>After the rejection, the coordinator proposed four ways forward in two days. The last was an ask that you could not read. At 05:56 on 28 October you said: “No. None of this. I cannot decipher what you are proposing here.”</li>
</ol></li>
<li><p><strong>The skills between the last good consolidation and the failed one</strong> were right but too thin. Fourteen commits say what each tool does. They do not say which job each tool fits.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The deeper cause.</strong> At 09:12 on 29 October you asked whether you had been “prompting a genie”. The coordinator answered at once: “The genie did not follow your wish literally. It followed a worker's report literally, and your words only as far as they named the tool.” It added: “The same pattern ran yesterday, with me. The account's worker proposed three tool changes; I relayed them to you without checking the tools.” You had seen that at 11:49 on 28 October: “you are committing the very same mistake that the original the Opus in full context when it caused the debacle.”</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Your other asks of 10:39 on 28 October.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The Haiku window ran from 16 to 19 October. In those days the coordinator ran on the Haiku model by accident. The Haiku window did not produce the weakened export check of row 816 of the gap ledger. The export check compares a component with its interface file. The gap ledger is the file of known gaps. Nor did the window produce the entries that the review found bent. All of them came later, under Opus. At 11:00 you accepted that: “Okay, so that was my misremembering.” You then asked who had raised row 816. The recheck found that your memory held in part. The batch's own reviewer raised row 816 on 23 October. Climb batch 22 then launched overnight at its default, and nobody sent you the question. A climb batch is a Workflow run of several code tasks that land together.</li>
<li>The exit review's records are one process in ten places. At 11:17 you said: “So yes, let's do the workers proposal. And create a README.” A README now names the process's six layers. Its open asks moved into the review queue as items 279 to 290. The review queue is the file of items that wait for your word.</li>
<li>One ask has no answer yet: whether the coordinator's delegation declined since the early Fable sessions. You placed it “once we recover from here”.</li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Point 1: your messages at 09:56:51 and 10:28:27 on 28 October; commit <code>d9ca11c4a</code>.</li>
<li>Point 2: <a href="@G@@PM@account.md">the account</a>, section 2. Cause 2: <a href="@G@.claude/skills/coordinator/references/record.md">record.md</a>, the record's rules for an entry. Cause 5: your message at 05:56:38 on 28 October.</li>
<li>Point 3: <a href="@G@@PM@account.md">the account</a>, section 4.</li>
<li>Point 4: your message at 09:12:17 and the coordinator's at 09:12:52, on 29 October; your message at 11:49:40 on 28 October.</li>
<li>Point 5: your message at 10:39:33 on 28 October. The Haiku window: <a href="@G@@EV@haiku-and-row-816.md">haiku-and-row-816.md</a>; your message at 11:00:40; <a href="@G@@EV@row-816-recheck.md">row-816-recheck.md</a>. The exit review's records: your message at 11:17:20; commit <code>41c3b3491</code>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="s3" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">Section 3</span>What was done</h2>

<section id="s3-tool" class="part sub">
<h3><span class="num">28 October, 11:30 to 21:40</span>Which tool</h3>
<ol class="points">
<li><p>Three Opus workers weighed the tools. Neither the clarity workflow nor the mining workflow fitted as designed. The mining workflow traces each entry to its sources. It could take the findings well only beside freshly run scouts. Scouts are Sonnet workers that fetch the exact words behind each entry. Then at 12:13 you corrected the premise: “It's about making the right decision that resolves the contradictions from the repository left by the original team.” Read so, the findings are readings of the team's sources, not traces of your words. Then the clarity workflow's steps fit, with short additions.</p></li>
<li><p>At 20:42 you said: “Yes, prepare the three additions and show me the wording.” At 20:55 you said: “Yes, apply the three edits and prepare the (trial?) run”. The coordinator put three additions into the tool's own briefs. The first is an input for the findings. The second lets a reason rest on the team's texts. The third checks each change against its finding's ground, the evidence that the finding rests on. The run took no brief of its own.</p></li>
<li><p>At 21:10 you said, on length: “soft cap 600 as already on tool, instruction for minimal change of the finding. [...] we'll judge if the content expands beyond reason.” At 21:12 you said: “But I think a pilot should be 3-4 entries, not 9.” The pilot became four entries, and the rest became two chunks of seven. At 21:40 you said “Yes to the five, go”, and the trap test gained five scenarios, its test cases.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="s3-pilot" class="part sub">
<h3><span class="num">28 October, to 01:29 on 29 October</span>The pilot twice, then the chunks overnight</h3>
<ol class="points">
<li><p>The first pilot run wrote the findings' grounds into the entries. At 22:19 you said: “I think the positions contain too much detail and references which should be the historical record and not in the position writing which should be kept timeless and referencing the FACTS on record where needed.” The coordinator put two more sentences into the tool's writer and checker. The checker is the tool's step that compares each text with its sources. The first sentence asks for the corrected reading as a decision with a pointer. The second puts the dates, counts, commits and file names in the history note. At 22:37 you said: “Yes. Re-run the pilot.”</p></li>
<li><p>The second pilot passed the coordinator's checks. At 23:09 you asked: “Can I trust you to judge the pilot and run the full thing of it pans out? [...] I guess I can afford a speculative run.” Both chunks ran overnight. Each chunk ran after the one before it had passed.</p></li>
<li><p>The result: 14 of the 18 entries changed. The 18 entries grew from 16,284 to 19,750 characters, by 21 percent. The trap test had nine scenarios. In eight of them, the new texts scored no lower than the old texts, on the better of two readings. The ninth scenario was about the library's <code>System</code> after the switch-over. <code>System</code> is the library's component that gives a program its arguments and its environment. In that scenario the new texts scored lower on both readings. The lower score traced to two new sentences. The checking worker chose the bar “better of two readings”, and the coordinator accepted it.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="s3-reading" class="part sub">
<h3><span class="num">29 October, 05:22 to 09:05</span>Your reading and the two sorts</h3>
<ol class="points">
<li><p>You left 16 comments on the corrections page from 05:22 to 07:58. Two of them turned the work. At 05:35 you wrote: “we might have a briefing issue that misdirects the signal into wrong part of the record”. At 05:49 you wrote: “Some of them belong to history, some to FACTS. Many are simply not a rewrite of the P-XX text!” At 07:18 you wrote: “It applied no judgment whether the finding is significant. [...] This is like all the letter of the law without any spirit of the law.”</p></li>
<li><p>An Opus worker sorted the 30 findings twice. The first sort was by home. Eight findings belong in the entry's text, seven in its history and six in FACTS, and nine need no change. The run had written into the texts the 13 findings whose home is the history or FACTS. The second sort was by weight. Eleven findings are consequential, 16 are minor, and three have no weight. Only four entries needed new words.</p></li>
<li><p>Three searches of the past settled three entries. The first was on P-27, extending the library. The second was on the compiler's <code>CompilerSystem</code> file. No decision covers that file's fate. Its question came up on 5 October, and nobody put it to you. The third was on P-146, the self-typed traits.</p></li>
<li><p>At 08:00 you summed up your reading with a picture. The coordinator read it as a shape sorter. The run had pushed every finding through the one hole it had, the entry's text.</p></li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="s3-landing" class="part sub">
<h3><span class="num">29 October, 09:05 to 13:31</span>The final writing and the landing</h3>
<ol class="points">
