<section id="s3-pilot" class="part sub">
<h3><span class="num">28 October, to 01:29 on 29 October</span>The pilot twice, then the chunks overnight</h3>
<ol class="points">
<li><p>The first pilot run wrote the findings' grounds into the entries. At 22:19 you said: “I think the positions contain too much detail and references which should be the historical record and not in the position writing which should be kept timeless and referencing the FACTS on record where needed.” The coordinator put two more sentences into the tool's writer and checker. The checker is the tool's step that compares each text with its sources. The first sentence asks for the corrected reading as a decision with a pointer. The second puts the dates, counts, commits and file names in the history note. At 22:37 you said: “Yes. Re-run the pilot.”</p></li>
<li><p>The second pilot passed the coordinator's checks. At 23:09 you asked: “Can I trust you to judge the pilot and run the full thing of it pans out? [...] I guess I can afford a speculative run.” Both chunks ran overnight. Each chunk ran after the one before it had passed.</p></li>
<li><p>The result: 14 of the 18 entries changed. The 18 entries grew from 16,284 to 19,750 characters, by 21 percent. The trap test had nine scenarios. In eight of them, the new texts scored no lower than the old texts, on the better of two readings. The ninth scenario was about the library's <code>System</code> after the switch-over. <code>System</code> is the library's component that gives a program its arguments and its environment. In that scenario the new texts scored lower on both readings. The lower score traced to two new sentences. The checking worker chose the bar “better of two readings”, and the coordinator accepted it.</p></li>
