<section id="s5" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">Section 5</span>What it taught</h2>
<ol class="points">
<li><p><strong>Right process, wrong brief.</strong> Every step up to the launch was right, as you said at 10:28 on 28 October. The run brief alone set the tool's rules aside. The hook is gone. Since then the clarity workflow has run four times, and each run took only arguments, with no brief of its own. The rule stands in the skill only on the skill branch so far.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>A worker's report is not the plan.</strong> At 09:12 on 29 October the coordinator proposed two steps for the skill writer. First, before acting on a report, say in three lines what it found, what it recommends, what the goal needs and where they differ. Second, when you name a tool or method, check from its own documents that it fits, and say so first. You did not answer the two steps directly. Your next message asked for the context analysis, at 09:14. My reading: the failure came three times in this post-mortem, in the run brief of 26 October, in the account's tool proposals that the coordinator relayed unchecked, and in the overnight run that took the findings list as the work.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>The design's evidence grounds a correction.</strong> The ground of an entry is the specification, the team's later word, the library and the code. Your words show what you decided. You said so at 12:13 on 28 October.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Each finding has a home.</strong> The home is the entry's text, its history, FACTS, or no change. The run's tool has one output, the entry's text. So the run wrote 21 findings into the texts, and only 8 of them belonged there. Sort by home before anyone writes.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Weigh significance first.</strong> Of 30 findings, four needed new words in an entry. You raised this at 07:18 on 29 October.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>A small pilot that you read is the only check of form that worked.</strong> The record check is the check that the tool record.py runs. The record check, the cold read and the trap test passed texts that you then rejected or had rewritten. That happened on 26 October and again on 29 October. Only your reading caught the length, the register, history in an entry and the wrong home. My reading: the trap test measures decisions, so it cannot be the gate for form.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>What made the good rewrite.</strong> At 13:01 on 29 October the coordinator said: “Your sixteen comments were the main one. [...] So the hard work of reading each entry was the content, and the sorting rule was the container. The run of the night before had the container wrong, and no amount of writing fixes that.” At 21:05 you said: “it was quite a lot of manual labor to go through all points of part one and give my feedback to each of it.” My reading: the result cost your reading time, and a sort by home and weight before the writing is what makes that reading shorter next time.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Light briefs, heavy delegation.</strong> At 07:37 on 28 October you said: “You are spending your reasoning effort, your context here, on micromanaging workers.” At 11:46 you said: “don't pick the reading list. Let them do it.” At 22:04 on 29 October you found the fault again, in a librarian's brief. It had 625 words, nine files, four reading steps and six time windows. At 21:05 you set the split. Sonnet workers fetch exact words. Opus workers turn them into a report. The coordinator reads the report.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Reground before each ask and launch.</strong> To reground is to say where the work stands, what the step must produce, and what it protects against. You asked for this at 12:18 on 28 October. You made this check yourself after 16 of the 84 manual compactions, mostly in late September. No skill states the check.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Guard the context.</strong> From 06:04 on 28 October to 09:15 on 29 October, the coordinator's context grew from 107 thousand to 693 thousand tokens. Of that context, 45 percent was the coordinator's own output. There were 25 turns that answered a page comment. Each of them took about four calls and 5 thousand tokens. At 12:35 on 29 October you asked for this as guidance in the next skill update.</p></li>
<li><p><strong>Recheck a disputed memory at the source.</strong> Twice your memory corrected the chain of evidence, at 09:56 and at 11:00 on 28 October. Both rechecks found that your memory held at least in part. At 11:00 you said: “I am again worried by the lossy compression of information from the process that we are using now.”</p></li>
</ol>
